### 0908 — Review: minimal ReluxNIOSSH fork accepted; two adapter-facing caveats (TASK-260715-nzdzv3)
- MILESTONE: Fork accepted → `done`. Reviewer independently re-ran every gate: `make validate-reluxniossh` (exact archive/license SHA-256, 16-file allowlist, 323 upstream XCTest + 10 Swift Testing, build), strict `swift format lint`, same-pin `conflict-test`, `make validate-core` (49 tests/build) — all exit 0. Default-path equivalence of the new adjust math to upstream verified analytically for even window sizes (default 1<<24); protected-byte boundary matches contract §10.1 (post-encode pre-write at `NIOSSHHandler.swift:266`, post-read pre-decode at `NIOSSHHandler.swift:205`); all outbound frames flow through the single `writeMessage` path.
- FINDING: During any KEX (client- or server-initiated) upstream's state machine rejects outbound `channelData` with `protocolViolation` (`SSHConnectionStateMachine.swift:1060-1076`), failing the child write promise. Pre-existing upstream behavior, unchanged by the fork — but automatic byte/time rekey makes the collision window likely under bulk traffic. The NIOSSH adapter (TASK-260715-1af33i) MUST gate/queue child writes on `rekeySnapshot.keyExchangeInProgress` / `NIOSSHRekeyStartedEvent`→`NIOSSHRekeySucceededEvent`; the fork exposes exactly this observability for the contract's "writes MAY suspend" clause.
- FINDING: `NIOSSHChannelWindowConfiguration` invariant is `remaining + buffered + delivered == initialWindowSize`, so remaining credit never grows past `initialWindowSize` even when `maximumWindowSize` is larger, and a `windowAdjustmentThreshold > initialWindowSize` (allowed by preconditions, which only check ≤ max) permanently withholds WINDOW_ADJUST — a stalled-channel footgun. Adapter policy must keep threshold ≤ initial (≤ half is sane). Cap semantics remain contract-correct: `remainingBefore + adjustment ≤ cap` always holds.
- NOTE: `.serverInitiated` rekey reason semantically means "remote-initiated" (a server-role handler labels client-triggered KEX the same way); cosmetic for the client-only tunnel. `requestRekey` in a non-active state fails the promise and also fires `errorCaught` — callers should not treat pre-active requests as recoverable no-ops.

### 0850 — Minimal ReluxNIOSSH window/rekey fork is reviewable (TASK-260715-nzdzv3)
- DECISION: The source fork is an independent SwiftPM package at `Dependencies/ReluxNIOSSH`, based byte-for-byte on SwiftNIO SSH `0.14.1` / `31cdc3c` before a 16-file allowlisted delta. The `NIOSSH` product/module and upstream 16 MiB/no-auto-rekey defaults remain source-compatible; `ReluxTunnelCore` has no dependency on the fork.
- IMPLEMENTATION: Child initial window/cap/adjustment policy is set through a normal pre-open channel option and observed through snapshots/events; returned credit is earned only after pipeline delivery. Client manual, per-direction protected-byte, and injected-monotonic-time rekeys share the existing KEX state machine, coalesce concurrent reasons/promises, reset generation counters on success, and observe server-initiated KEX without product reflection or a test-only API.
- VERIFICATION: The audited archive SHA-256 (`0b135087…d06e6a`) and Apache-2.0 license SHA-256 (`cfc7749b…23d30`) reproduce. The full fork suite passes 323 upstream XCTest cases plus 10 deterministic Swift Testing cases; strict Swift format lint, fork debug build, 49 root tests, root build, exact-delta verification, and same-pin conflict preflight pass. The patch manifest, unified upstream diff, license/provenance review, rebase procedure, conflict command, and upstreaming split are task outcomes.

### 0821 — Candidate-neutral SSH conformance contract closes the shared-surface gap (TASK-260715-2ny6z4)
- DECISION: Both engine adapters use one serialized, type-erased `ReluxTunnelCore` boundary with mandatory raw host-key policy evaluation before credential access, bounded partial writes, independent byte-stream halves, capped observable per-channel receive credit, automatic byte/time plus explicit/server rekey, keepalive, stable errors, schema-v1 metrics/events, and privacy-safe opaque lane identity. ADR-014 remains open; unsupported mandatory behavior is a red matrix row, never a candidate-specific skip.
- FINDING: The existing `SSHContracts.swift` is intentionally only an M0 boundary skeleton and no board item owned implementation of the reviewed semantic surface before both adapters and the common suite. Created atomic TASK-260720-100wu6 and linked it between this contract and TASK-260715-1af33i, TASK-260715-1ozsb6, and TASK-260715-2d3g5e; the independent NIOSSH fork continues to consume this contract directly.
- VERIFICATION: The contract defines every operation, state, timeout/cancellation scope, error, metric, privacy rule, and E-ALGO/E-HOSTAUTH/E-CHANNELS/E-BACKPRESSURE/E-WINDOW/E-REKEY/E-KEEPALIVE/E-CANCEL/E-SCALE/E-ERRORS/E-METRICS-PRIVACY/E-INJECTION row. Three task-scoped PlantUML sources pass syntax checking and their lifecycle, pre-auth host verification, and channel/window/rekey SVG renders were visually inspected.

### 0812 — Review: SSH engine audit accepted after full independent fact-check (TASK-260715-28ok1k)
- MILESTONE: Audit accepted → `done`. Every load-bearing claim reproduced from upstream: tag→commit pins for both candidates plus all six transitive deps (ls-remote), all three archive SHA-256 and both license hashes (fresh downloads), 16 MiB window `SSHChannelMultiplexer.swift:211`, internal `_rekey()` `NIOSSHHandler.swift:511`, libssh2 window APIs and direct-tcpip hardcode `src/channel.c:384,457`, unexported reexchange `src/packet.c:1369`, version-macro anomaly, six CVE fix commits confirmed ancestors of `a343024`, GHSA advisories via API, exactly 679 libssh2 commits/12mo.
- FINDING: swift-nio carries two additional 2026 medium HTTP-path advisories (GHSA-rj37-6j9x-74q6, GHSA-cq87-8r7h-962v) not enumerated in the audit; both are patched by the pinned `2.101.3`, so the security-baseline conclusion stands. E-SUPPLYCHAIN re-audit will re-enumerate.
- NOTE: Audit's NIOSSH release dates are tag-commit dates, not GitHub `published_at`; tag dates match upstream exactly, no correction needed. Reviewer evidence: board resource `TASK-260715-28ok1k_review-verdict.md`.

### 0759 — SSH engine source audit pins fixed baselines without selecting an engine (TASK-260715-28ok1k)
- SECURITY: SwiftNIO SSH is pinned to `0.14.1` / `31cdc3c`, which fixes critical CVE-2026-43798, with resolved Swift Crypto `4.5.1` and SwiftNIO `2.101.3` security-patched transitives. libssh2 release `1.11.1` is not a clean baseline because six advisories were fixed after release; the candidate is pinned to untagged `1.11.2_DEV` commit `a343024` containing all six fixes, with OpenSSL `3.5.7`. Re-audit on a signed libssh2 1.11.2+ release is mandatory.
- FINDING: SwiftNIO SSH hardcodes each child receive-window target to 16 MiB and exposes client `_rekey()` only internally. libssh2 exposes generic per-channel initial/remaining/adjust APIs, but its direct-tcpip helper fixes the 2 MiB default and it likewise has no public client-rekey API or automatic byte/time threshold.
- DECISION: ADR-014 remains open. Both candidates retain identical host-key, window, sustained 5 GiB rekey, scale (through 1,000 channels), cancellation, algorithm, extension-build, memory, and physical-size gates. Unknowns are named experiments in `.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md`; no missing capability was waived.
- RISK: libssh2 master issue #2023 contains an unresolved AEAD correctness report whose specific leak interpretation is disputed. Treat it as an ASan/packet-content experiment, not a proven leak or a reason to prejudge the engine.

### 0748 — Review: reachable shutdown deadlock in HEV quit path (TASK-260715-1vv52g)
- FINDING: `HEVDescriptorBorrowHandle.requestStop()` (`Sources/ReluxTunnelNativeAdapter/HEVIntegration.swift:374`) calls `hev_socks5_tunnel_quit()` unconditionally. Pinned upstream `hev_socks5_tunnel_stop` (`src/hev-socks5-tunnel.c:695`) busy-waits forever for `event_fds[1] >= 0`; `event_task_fini` resets both fds to `-1` when main returns, and init-failure returns (-1…-5) never create them.
- ROOT CAUSE: Any ordering where HEV main returns before `requestStop()` — init failure, or the contract-modeled "unexpected HEV main return while active" — makes `supervisedCleanup` (`Sources/ReluxTunnelCore/PacketFlowBridge.swift:1017`) invoke quit after fini: infinite 100 ms `usleep` loop on a cooperative executor thread; provider `stop()` never returns, descriptors never close. Plain `stop()` racing a just-died HEV hits the same path.
- FINDING: Masked in tests because fake runtimes' `requestStop` never blocks and every lifecycle test stops HEV before its main returns.
- NOTE: A residual quit-vs-spontaneous-return race (write-after-close/assert inside HEV) is inherent upstream — its own `hev-jni.c` shares it — and is out of fixable scope without patching; the guard narrows the window to that inherent case.
- REWORK: `requestStop()` now skips upstream quit after the joined main call has returned while preserving listener shutdown and stop metrics. Tests cover both main-return-first and stop-first orderings and fail if quit lands after return.
- REWORK: The loopback listener fd is closed exactly once by its dispatch-source cancel handler on `listenerQueue`; `stop()` waits for cancellation before returning, eliminating the accept-loop fd-reuse window.
- VERIFICATION: Strict swift-format, 49 Swift tests, `swift build`, 8 HEV tests under ThreadSanitizer, and `make validate-native` all pass. The residual quit-vs-spontaneous-return race remains upstream-inherent and would require a forbidden HEV patch; the return guard narrows exposure to that concurrent window.

### 0702 — Unmodified HEV/lwIP bridge integration (TASK-260715-1vv52g)
- DECISION: The real `DescriptorBorrowConsumer` runs pinned HEV `ad760049` on exactly one dedicated pthread, calls the public quit API once, joins before return, and never closes or duplicates bridge endpoint B. A process-wide lease protects HEV's global C state.
- DECISION: HEV's upstream SOCKS connection is restricted to an IPv4-loopback listener with fresh per-run RFC 1929 credentials. Only an authenticated owned channel reaches the injected adapter; pending-authentication count and timeout are caller inputs, and an external no-auth ingress test is rejected before the seam.
- VERIFICATION: The emitted in-memory YAML records caller MTU, `udp: tcp`, task stack 24576, TCP buffer 4096, `udp-copy-buffer-nums: 2`, and max sessions 1200. The generator rejects any stack input the pinned parser would silently raise.
- VERIFICATION: The checksum-locked static XCFramework is a SwiftPM binary target for the shared native adapter and all provider/harness graphs. Generated and statically embedded HEV/core/task-system/lwIP notices are byte-identical and trace every manifest revision; the stripped harness audit requires HEV main/quit/stats symbols.
- ANOMALY: Darwin accepted sockets inherited nonblocking behavior during the split RFC 1929 exchange; explicitly restoring blocking mode fixed a deterministic transient-`EAGAIN` authentication rejection without retries or sleeps.

### 0634 — HEV low-memory baseline is not effective as recorded (TASK-260715-1vv52g)
- BLOCKER: Pinned unmodified HEV `ad760049` silently raises the requested `task-stack-size: 24576` to `35480`: `hev-config.c` computes `TASK_STACK_SIZE (20480) + max(tcp-buffer-size (4096), UDP_BUF_SIZE (1500) * udp-copy-buffer-nums (default 10))` for every UDP mode, including `socks5.udp: tcp`.
- VERIFICATION: A native probe linked directly against the checksum-verified rebuilt XCFramework reports `task-stack-size=35480`, `tcp-buffer-size=4096`, `udp-copy-buffer-nums=10`, and `max-session-count=1200`; evidence is `.temp/TASK-260715-1vv52g/hev-effective-config-probe-01.log`.
- DECISION NEEDED: Either approve an additional injectable `udp-copy-buffer-nums` baseline of `1` or `2` so the effective stack remains 24576, explicitly accept 35480 as the effective stack while retaining 24576 only as an input, or revise the no-patch constraint. Recommendation: approve `2` as an explicit M0 measurement input; do not patch HEV or claim that 24576 is effective while the parser overrides it.

