 ## 2026-07-21
 
+### 0140 — M1 coordinator closes the startup-completion ownership race (TASK-260715-3tlgwm)
+- REGRESSION: A mandatory-health callback could enter after the tracked startup child returned but before the outer `start()` continuation resumed. Because `startupTask` was still retained, the callback deferred cleanup; the outer continuation then cleared the handle and returned success without revalidating actor state, leaving the generation parked in stopping with retained resources.
+- FIX: After joining the startup child, `start()` now rechecks caller cancellation, environment cancellation, lifecycle ownership, and termination while actor-isolated before atomically clearing `startupTask` and returning. A callback that wins the handoff therefore forces the error/cleanup path; a callback that arrives after the atomic handoff observes no startup owner and starts cleanup itself.
+- COVERAGE: A package-internal async handoff hook, following the existing core test-hook pattern, deterministically injects current-generation DNS health loss after child completion and independently pauses the same boundary for caller cancellation. Neither test issues a masking `stop()`; both assert no capability revival, exactly-once reverse cleanup, a zero external resource ledger, and the coordinator baseline footprint.
+- VERIFICATION: The focused suite passes 13 tests normally and under Thread Sanitizer. `make validate-core` passes boundary/native checks, 205 tests in 23 suites, and post-test `swift build`; strict recursive Swift format lint, `git diff --check`, and board validation pass.
+
+### 0120 — M1 coordinator makes settings uncertainty and cleanup ordering explicit (TASK-260715-3tlgwm)
+- IMPLEMENTATION: `TunnelRuntimeCoordinator` owns one actor-serialized generation and a tracked startup task. It retains each successful acquisition before the next cancellation check, rejects repeat starts, discards mismatched-generation health events, and coalesces caller cancellation, provider stop, startup failure, and mandatory health loss through one shielded cleanup task.
+- SAFETY: Settings are requested only after validated configuration, authenticated SSH, TCP readiness, SSH-only safe DNS readiness, pure packet-plane preparation, and mandatory health checks. Unknown apply failures are conservatively treated as an uncertain commit and cleared; definite non-commit skips clear. A clear failure publishes TCP/DNS false while truthfully retaining `routesInstalled=true` and `routeState=clearFailed`.
+- CLEANUP: Teardown closes admission, stops packet reads/native work, clears settings when required, then stops DNS, TCP, and SSH in reverse acquisition order. The coordinator releases configuration references and all tracked task/resource handles; repeated stop calls join the same cleanup.
+- VERIFICATION: Ten injected startup failures, seven stop/cancellation boundaries, caller cancellation, stale callbacks, 32 concurrent stops, clear failure, snapshot ordering, and 100 generations pass in normal and Thread Sanitizer runs. `make validate-core` passes boundary/native checks, 203 tests in 23 suites, and the post-test build; strict repository Swift format lint, `git diff --check`, and board validation pass.
+
 ### 0053 — Runtime diagnostics ingestion is bounded and non-blocking (TASK-260715-1i49fm)
 - REWORK: Component recorders now reserve one of 256 pending typed-update slots with a zero-timeout semaphore and enqueue on a private serial diagnostics lane. Snapshot construction can serialize on that lane without holding anything packet or SSH executors need; saturation increments one generation-scoped aggregate and retains no rejected label or value.
 - CARDINALITY: Diagnostics errors are constrained to a reviewed 10-case `RuntimeDiagnosticErrorCode` catalog with a fixed domain per code. Decoded unknown or domain-mismatched tokens are counted and discarded before enqueue, closing the unstable-label path.

