 ## 2026-07-21
 
+### 0150 — M1 coordinator terminally consumes a stop-before-start generation (TASK-260715-3tlgwm)
+- REGRESSION: `stop()` treated the coordinator's initial disconnected state as an idempotent no-op, so it retained the profile reference and left the one-shot generation startable after stop returned.
+- FIX: The lifecycle now models one `generationConsumed` invariant shared by start and stop. A winning pre-start stop records termination, enters stopping, and runs the existing single cleanup owner; this releases the configuration reference before returning and makes every later start reject with `generationAlreadyConsumed`.
+- COVERAGE: Deterministic direct and concurrent stop-wins tests assert one stopping-to-disconnected publication cycle, repeat-stop idempotency, no configuration load/settings/capability publication, no external resources, and the internal baseline footprint. The concurrent test pauses snapshot publication after the terminal state is recorded, allowing a competing start to enter during the in-flight stop without a production test hook.
+- VERIFICATION: The focused suite passes 15 tests normally and under Thread Sanitizer. `make validate-core` passes boundary/native checks, 207 tests in 23 suites, and post-test `swift build`; strict recursive Swift format lint, tracked/untracked whitespace checks, and board validation pass.
+
 ### 0140 — M1 coordinator closes the startup-completion ownership race (TASK-260715-3tlgwm)
 - REGRESSION: A mandatory-health callback could enter after the tracked startup child returned but before the outer `start()` continuation resumed. Because `startupTask` was still retained, the callback deferred cleanup; the outer continuation then cleared the handle and returned success without revalidating actor state, leaving the generation parked in stopping with retained resources.
 - FIX: After joining the startup child, `start()` now rechecks caller cancellation, environment cancellation, lifecycle ownership, and termination while actor-isolated before atomically clearing `startupTask` and returning. A callback that wins the handoff therefore forces the error/cleanup path; a callback that arrives after the atomic handoff observes no startup owner and starts cleanup itself.
